<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peterborough</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Medeshamstede</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peterborough is a cathedral city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridgeshire">Cambridgeshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-nene">River Nene</a>. It grew around the abbey of Medeshamstede, founded in the mid-7th century under Mercian royal patronage, destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> around 870 and refounded in the 10th century as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peterborough-abbey">Peterborough Abbey</a>. Its Norman abbey church became a cathedral in 1541, and a monk there continued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> into the 12th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peterborough.jpg" alt="Peterborough" width="320" height="303" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NotFromUtrecht · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Peterborough_Cathedral_March_2010.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Peterborough lies 74 miles (119 km) north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-nene">River Nene</a>, on the western edge of the Fens. The land is flat and in places below sea level; Holme Fen, just south of the city, is said to be the lowest point on land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The city has been called the Gateway to the Fens. Settlement began here because the Nene creates permanently drained land at this point. The Oxford Clay, dug in brick pits, has yielded fossils of Jurassic marine reptiles and, in 1898, a sauropod skeleton later named Cetiosauriscus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Flag Fen site east of the city centre shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> settlement and what is thought to be religious activity. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> founded a fortified garrison town at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rochester-kent">Durobrivae</a>, now Water Newton, around the middle of the 1st century CE, and a large fort at Longthorpe, perhaps as early as 44 to 48 CE, built for about 3,000 soldiers. The area produced Nene Valley Ware, traded as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonine-wall">Antonine Wall</a>.</p><p>The original town was the monastery of Medeshamstede, founded in 655. From the late tenth century the name became Burgh, possibly after Abbot Kenulf built a defensive wall around the abbey, which was dedicated to Saint Peter; this became Peterborough. The present cathedral was rebuilt between 1118 and 1238. When the abbey church became a cathedral in 1541 the last abbot became the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and the town was deemed a city. Catherine of Aragon and Mary, Queen of Scots were buried there, though Mary's remains were later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> by her son James I.</p><p>The Great Northern Railway's line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to York, opened in 1850, turned a market town into an industrial centre known for brickworks and engineering. The Great Fire of Peterborough destroyed much of the old centre on 22 August 1956, and the city was designated a new town in the 1960s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 192,178 people in the urban area, and the city is a unitary authority within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridgeshire">Cambridgeshire</a>. Peterborough Cathedral, one of the most intact large Norman buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, is known for its early English Gothic west front with three great arches. The Nene Viaduct of 1847, by Sir William and Joseph Cubitt, is the oldest surviving cast-iron railway bridge in the country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peterborough” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christianity arrived after Penda's death through Irish-trained missionaries from Northumbria; Chad established the see of Lichfield in 669, and monasteries such as Repton, Medeshamstede (<b>Peterborough</b>) and Breedon flourished.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>During the civil war in England known as the Anarchy (1138–1153) barons built castles and fought for rival claimants, and the <b>Peterborough</b> Chronicle reported men saying openly that Christ and his saints slept.</blockquote><a href="/government/feudalism">Feudalism · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition links Peada to the foundation of Medeshamstede, later <b>Peterborough</b> Abbey.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-middle-angles">Kingdom of the Middle Angles</a></li></ul></section>
